    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7ef31c92",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: they_have_no_where_to_live_makes_special_camp_spots_just_for_them; hasOverallIntent: Hateful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a group of young Jewish boys in traditional attire, huddled together looking at a document. The overlaid text reads 'they have no where to live' and 'makes special camp spots just for them.' This juxtaposition implies that these children are being treated as a burden or a special case requiring separate accommodations, which is a harmful and stereotypical portrayal. The meme uses the visual of vulnerable children to evoke sympathy while simultaneously suggesting they are an 'extra' or 'problematic' group that needs to be segregated. This is a hateful message that targets Jewish children by implying they are an unnecessary or burdensome group requiring special, isolated treatment, which is a form of antisemitic tropes that portray Jewish people as a burden on society or as needing special, separate treatment. The meme is designed to provoke discomfort or reinforce negative stereotypes about Jewish communities, particularly by implying that their existence or needs are an inconvenience or a special case that requires exclusion from normal societal structures.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
